    { s: "What's a freedom-to-operate analysis?", p: "The scariest memo any inventor asks for." },
    { s: "Why did the FTO opinion feel forever?", p: "Every claim, every product, every geography — the matrix explodes." },
    { s: "What did in-house counsel prefer over formal opinions?", p: "Detailed clearance searches with attorney summaries — less exposure, more coverage." },
    { s: "Why did trade secrets return to fashion?", p: "Post-Alice, some inventions had nowhere better to go." },
    { s: "What's the Defend Trade Secrets Act?", p: "Federal cause of action, 2016. Trade secrets went national." },
    { s: "Why did the DTSA include ex parte seizure?", p: "For flight risks with trade-secret files on a laptop." },
    { s: "What did the whistleblower immunity clause do?", p: "Protect employees who disclose trade secrets to lawyers or the government in confidence." },
    { s: "Why did every NDA suddenly cite 18 U.S.C. § 1833(b)?", p: "Because failing to notify employees limits your DTSA remedies." },
    { s: "What's a covenant not to compete?", p: "Sometimes trade-secret protection. Sometimes labor-mobility restraint. State law decides which." },
    { s: "Why did the FTC try to ban non-competes?", p: "Because it thought most were unnecessary and anticompetitive." },
    { s: "What survived after the FTC ban was enjoined?", p: "State-by-state law. California vs. Florida. Same words, opposite outcomes." },
    { s: "What did the DOJ v. patents look like in 2024?", p: "Standard-essential patents, FRAND rate cases, and the occasional antitrust warning shot." },
    { s: "Why did SEP holders and standards implementers stop trusting each other?", p: "Because 'reasonable' was defined by whoever spoke last." },
    { s: "What did the Federal Circuit tell everyone in 2024?", p: "'You keep petitioning us to fix the same three doctrines. We're not going to.'" },
    { s: "Why did AI-generated inventions raise §115 questions?", p: "Because 'inventor' means a natural person — and the USPTO said so." },
    { s: "What's the Thaler v. Vidal takeaway?", p: "'DABUS may have invented it, but only a human can be named on the application.'" },
    { s: "Why did AI-assisted invention guidance take so long?", p: "Because 'significantly contributed' is subjective at every layer." },
    { s: "What did the USPTO's 2024 AI guidance require?", p: "A natural person had to make a significant contribution to each claim. Prompts alone aren't enough." },
    { s: "Why did the joint-inventorship analysis get harder with AI?", p: "Because your prompt engineer might have contributed more than the model. Or less. Nobody's sure." },
    { s: "What did the biggest patent law firms do in 2024?", p: "Started AI-invention practice groups. Not sure what to charge for them yet." },
  ];

  // ── Content: Patent of the Day — real patents, famous and absurd ──
  var PATENTS = [
    { no: "US 6,469", y: 1849, t: "Buoying Vessels Over Shoals", s: "Abraham Lincoln's device for lifting riverboats off sandbars — making him the only US president with a patent. It was never manufactured, but the model he whittled is in the Smithsonian." },
    { no: "US 174,465", y: 1876, t: "Improvement in Telegraphy", s: "Alexander Graham Bell's telephone patent — often called the most valuable patent ever issued. Bell's filing famously beat Elisha Gray's caveat for a similar invention at the Patent Office the very same day." },
    { no: "US 223,898", y: 1880, t: "Electric Lamp", s: "Edison's incandescent bulb with a carbonized filament. The patent survived years of litigation that established Edison didn't invent the lightbulb so much as the first one that actually worked — and the system to power it." },
    { no: "US 2,717,437", y: 1955, t: "Velvet Type Fabric (Velcro)", s: "George de Mestral examined the burrs stuck to his dog after a walk under a microscope, saw thousands of tiny hooks, and spent a decade making 'hook and loop' fasteners practical. The patent expired in 1978 — which is why everyone makes it now." },
    { no: "US 2,415,012", y: 1947, t: "Toy and Process of Use (Slinky)", s: "Naval engineer Richard James watched a torsion spring walk off a shelf and patented a 'helical spring toy.' Three hundred million Slinkys later, it's a lesson in claiming the use, not just the thing." },
    { no: "US 3,691,140", y: 1972, t: "Acrylate Copolymer Microspheres (Post-it)", s: "Spencer Silver patented a 'low-tack' adhesive nobody at 3M could find a use for — until Art Fry needed a bookmark that wouldn't fall out of his hymnal. The product launched twelve years after the patent." },
    { no: "US 3,351,836", y: 1967, t: "Intermittent Windshield Wiper", s: "Robert Kearns pitched his wiper to Ford, was turned away, then watched it appear on their cars. His decades-long infringement crusade won ~$30M and became the film 'Flash of Genius' — the patent system's great cautionary epic." },
    { no: "US 5,960,411", y: 1999, t: "1-Click Ordering", s: "Amazon's patent on buying with a single click — the poster child of the business-method patent era. Apple licensed it for the iTunes store rather than fight. It expired in 2017, and 'Buy Now' buttons bloomed everywhere overnight." },
    { no: "US 6,004,596", y: 1999, t: "Sealed Crustless Sandwich", s: "Smucker's patent behind Uncrustables. When they sued a small Michigan grocer over crimped PB&J, the resulting reexamination shredded the claims — prior art included, roughly, every grandmother." },
    { no: "AU 2001100012", y: 2001, t: "Circular Transportation Facilitation Device", s: "Australian patent attorney John Keogh patented the wheel to prove the new unexamined 'innovation patent' system would grant anything. IP Australia was not amused; the Ig Nobel committee was." },
    { no: "US 221,855", y: 1879, t: "Improvement in Fire-Escapes", s: "A parachute worn as a hat, paired with padded overshoes for landing. Benjamin Oppenheimer's drawing of a serene gentleman plummeting past a burning building remains one of the Patent Office's great artworks." },
    { no: "US 5,443,036", y: 1995, t: "Method of Exercising a Cat", s: "A granted patent on pointing a laser at the floor so a cat chases the dot. Exhibit A in every conversation about examination quality — and prior art known to every cat owner since the invention of the flashlight." },
    { no: "US 4,022,227", y: 1977, t: "Method of Concealing Partial Baldness", s: "A father-and-son team patented the comb-over: divide the remaining hair into three sections, sweep strategically. It won an Ig Nobel and immortality in §101 lectures." },
    { no: "US 6,360,693", y: 2002, t: "Animal Toy", s: "An 'apparatus for animal amusement' that is, on any fair reading of the claims, a stick. The USPTO later ordered reexamination. Dogs declined to comment on the validity of the prior art." },
    { no: "US 6,637,447", y: 2003, t: "Beerbrella", s: "A miniature umbrella that clips to a beer bottle to shade your beverage. Claim 1 is fully enabled, entirely novel at filing, and a standing reminder that patentable and profitable are different questions." },
    { no: "US 748,626", y: 1904, t: "The Landlord's Game", s: "Lizzie Magie patented the board game that became Monopoly — designed, ironically, to teach the evils of monopolies. Parker Brothers later bought her patent for about $500 and credited Charles Darrow instead." },
    { no: "US 72,X", y: 1794, t: "Cotton Gin", s: "Eli Whitney's patent for separating cotton fiber from seed reshaped the American South's economy — and was infringed so widely that Whitney barely made a dollar from it. The lesson every startup relearns: a patent you can't enforce is just paper." },
    { no: "US 3,633", y: 1844, t: "The Vulcanization of India-Rubber", s: "Charles Goodyear stumbled onto sulfur-and-heat vulcanization in a stove accident. He spent his life in and out of debtor's prison enforcing the patent, dying $200,000 in debt. The tire company that bore his name was founded decades after his death." },
    { no: "US 8,294", y: 1851, t: "Improvement in Fastening for Suspender-Ends", s: "Elias Howe's separate patent covering a slide-fastener concept anticipated the zipper by decades. Gideon Sundback perfected the modern zipper in 1913 — a classic case of patented, unmarketed, and eclipsed." },
    { no: "US 6,281", y: 1849, t: "Improvement in Safety Elevators", s: "Elisha Otis's safety brake — a spring-loaded ratchet that clamped the guide rails if the cable snapped — didn't sell until he cut the rope himself at the 1854 World's Fair. Skyscrapers followed." },
    { no: "US 78,317", y: 1868, t: "Type-Writing Machine", s: "Christopher Sholes's QWERTY layout was designed to separate common letter pairs so mechanical typebars wouldn't jam. The keyboard survived the jam problem by a century — and every device since." },
    { no: "US 78,015", y: 1868, t: "Wire-Fence", s: "Joseph Glidden's barbed wire ended the open-range West more effectively than any legislation. His victory over rival Isaac Ellwood cemented one of the most-litigated patents in US history." },
    { no: "US 174,465", y: 1876, t: "Improvement in Telegraphy — Bell's Second Patent", s: "The 1876 filing wasn't Bell's only telephone patent; US 186,787 (1877) covered the receiver. Both survived hundreds of infringement suits — the most successful patent enforcement campaign of the 19th century." },
    { no: "US 200,521", y: 1878, t: "Phonograph or Speaking-Machine", s: "Edison's foil-cylinder recorder played back the human voice — the first machine that could. He listed telephone-message dictation and 'the record of dying utterances of family members' among its uses." },
    { no: "US 227,229", y: 1880, t: "Method of Preparing Photographic Emulsion", s: "George Eastman's dry-plate gelatin process replaced messy wet-collodion photography. He built the Kodak empire around a slogan he trademarked before the camera even shipped: 'You press the button, we do the rest.'" },
    { no: "US 335,159", y: 1886, t: "System of Electrical Distribution", s: "Nikola Tesla's polyphase AC system won the War of the Currents. Westinghouse bought the patents; Tesla later tore up his royalty contract to save the company from bankruptcy — walking away from what would have been billions." },
    { no: "US 388,170", y: 1888, t: "Alternating-Current Motor", s: "Tesla's induction motor patent — the invention that made AC power distribution practical. Every appliance with an AC motor still traces to this filing." },
    { no: "US 549,160", y: 1895, t: "Gas Engine", s: "The Selden patent claimed to cover any gasoline-powered automobile. Ford spent eight years and hundreds of thousands fighting it — and won on appeal in 1911, freeing the American auto industry from a nonpracticing entity a century before the term existed." },
    { no: "US 586,193", y: 1897, t: "Transmission of Signals by Wireless", s: "Marconi's radiotelegraphy patent. The Supreme Court revoked its US validity in 1943 (Marconi Wireless v. US), holding Tesla, Lodge, and Stone had prior art — one of the great posthumous vindications in patent history." },
    { no: "US 613,809", y: 1898, t: "Method of and Apparatus for Controlling Mechanism of Moving Vessels", s: "Tesla's demonstration of a radio-controlled boat at Madison Square Garden. He proposed it as a torpedo. Nobody funded it. The technology waited half a century for the drone age." },
    { no: "US 686,046", y: 1901, t: "Automobile with Front-Wheel Drive", s: "Walter Christie's front-wheel-drive car patent influenced automotive engineering for decades. Christie's later T3 tank suspension design fed directly into the Soviet T-34 — the paper trail runs through Detroit to Stalingrad." },
    { no: "US 745,157", y: 1903, t: "Ferric-Aluminum Alloy — Radar Foundations", s: "Christian Hülsmeyer's Telemobiloscope was the first radio-echo obstacle detector — the direct ancestor of radar. His demonstrations impressed no one. Radar was reinvented, urgently, in the late 1930s." },
    { no: "US 821,393", y: 1906, t: "Flying Machine", s: "The Wright brothers' patent claimed 'wing-warping' for lateral control. Curtiss's ailerons became the workaround — and the case dragged on until WWI forced a government-mandated patent pool in 1917 to make American planes possible." },
    { no: "US 942,809", y: 1909, t: "Manufacture of Radiators", s: "Willis Carrier's air-conditioning patents (this one and US 1,172,784) — the invention that made summer productivity possible, movie theaters profitable, and the American South economically viable at scale." },
    { no: "US 1,242,872", y: 1917, t: "Fastener", s: "Gideon Sundback's 'Separable Fastener' — the modern zipper. It took another decade before B.F. Goodrich named it 'zipper' after the sound their overshoes made. Language followed function." },
    { no: "US 1,203,190", y: 1916, t: "Sanitary Method for Preserving Milk", s: "Clarence Birdseye's flash-freezing patents transformed food preservation. He sold the technology to General Foods for $22 million in 1929 — a fortune that Depression-era investors nearly forced him to accept in stock." },
    { no: "US 1,647,209", y: 1927, t: "Airplane", s: "Charles Lindbergh's Spirit of St. Louis wasn't patented, but Ryan Airlines patented refinements. The plane's fame outstripped every intellectual-property document ever filed on it." },
    { no: "US 1,773,980", y: 1930, t: "Television System", s: "Philo Farnsworth's electronic television — the man RCA fought for a decade rather than pay. He finally collected a million-dollar royalty in 1939, after which David Sarnoff's marketing machine buried his name in favor of Zworykin's." },
    { no: "US 1,745,175", y: 1930, t: "Method of Manufacturing Electric Capacitors", s: "Julius Lilienfeld's transistor concept — 17 years before Bell Labs. Lilienfeld's patent used solid-state control of current, but no working device was built. Bell Labs' 1947 breakthrough had to design around it." },
    { no: "US 2,140,132", y: 1938, t: "Fluorescent Lamp", s: "Edmund Germer's high-pressure fluorescent tube — GE bought his patent and made the fluorescent lamp a global standard. Every office in the 20th century paid Germer, indirectly." },
    { no: "US 2,168,703", y: 1939, t: "Fabric-Like Material — Nylon", s: "Wallace Carothers's polyamide fiber patent (with DuPont) launched synthetic fabrics. Carothers died before nylon stockings sold their first pair; his suicide two years before the product launch is one of the tragic footnotes of chemistry." },
    { no: "US 2,158,564", y: 1939, t: "Chair", s: "Charles Eames's molded plywood chair — patented before he became a design icon. Herman Miller has been paying variations of this royalty for 80 years." },
    { no: "US 2,292,387", y: 1942, t: "Secret Communication System", s: "Hedy Lamarr and George Antheil's frequency-hopping patent for radio-controlled torpedoes. The Navy shelved it as 'too complicated.' Bluetooth, Wi-Fi, and CDMA rediscovered it half a century later — none of which paid the estate a dime." },
    { no: "US 2,297,691", y: 1942, t: "Electrophotography", s: "Chester Carlson's xerography patent. Twenty companies rejected it. Haloid bought it in 1947, renamed itself Xerox in 1961, and became one of the most valuable patent bets in industrial history." },
    { no: "US 2,435,486", y: 1948, t: "Microwave Cooking Apparatus", s: "Percy Spencer, a Raytheon engineer, noticed a chocolate bar melting in his pocket near a magnetron. The first commercial Radarange stood six feet tall and cost the equivalent of $50,000. Countertop models took another 20 years." },
    { no: "US 2,524,035", y: 1950, t: "Three-Electrode Circuit Element — Transistor", s: "William Shockley's junction transistor patent — the device that displaced the vacuum tube and made every subsequent semiconductor. Bardeen and Brattain got the point-contact patent (US 2,524,033); the trio shared the 1956 Nobel Prize." },
    { no: "US 2,569,347", y: 1951, t: "Photography by Reflection", s: "Edwin Land's instant photography — the Polaroid patent. The company built an empire on cameras that developed prints in sixty seconds. When Kodak launched a competing instant camera in 1976, Land sued and won $909 million — the largest patent verdict of its era." },
    { no: "US 2,714,890", y: 1955, t: "Zipper-Type Package Closure", s: "Rip 'n' Zip: the resealable plastic bag closure. Robbins and Fischer's design won the war of the zip-top bag, and eventually became the Ziploc that made Dow Chemical billions." },
    { no: "US 2,752,624", y: 1956, t: "Bar Code Reader", s: "Joseph Woodland and Bernard Silver's original bar-code patent (bull's-eye version). Retail waited two decades for lasers and computer processing cheap enough to make it work. Woodland got his Medal of Technology in 1992 — Silver had died in 1963." },
    { no: "US 3,073,516", y: 1963, t: "Miniaturized Electronic Circuit", s: "Jack Kilby's integrated circuit patent for Texas Instruments. Robert Noyce's competing IC patent (US 2,981,877) went to Fairchild Semiconductor. The two shared credit — and Kilby got the 2000 Nobel Prize after Noyce's death made him ineligible." },
    { no: "US 3,138,743", y: 1964, t: "Miniaturized Electronic Circuits — Noyce", s: "Robert Noyce's monolithic integrated circuit — the planar process that made Silicon Valley possible. Intel, Fairchild, and every semiconductor company since owe him." },
    { no: "US 3,150,232", y: 1964, t: "Portable Radio Transceiver", s: "Motorola's handheld radio patent, the seed of the mobile phone. Martin Cooper's 1973 demo call from a Manhattan sidewalk to Bell Labs was made on Motorola's DynaTAC prototype — a two-pound brick that became a category." },
    { no: "US 3,449,750", y: 1969, t: "Duplex Digital Data Communication System", s: "The modem patent by John van Geen and John Rife. Every dial-up connection paid tribute to this filing, all the way to the fax machine's final day." },
    { no: "US 3,541,541", y: 1970, t: "X-Y Position Indicator for a Display System", s: "Doug Engelbart's computer mouse patent — filed in 1967, granted in 1970. Stanford Research Institute licensed it to Apple for $40,000. The mouse became universal; SRI made no fortune." },
    { no: "US 3,713,148", y: 1973, t: "Transponder Apparatus and System — RFID Origins", s: "Mario Cardullo's active RFID transponder patent — the ancestor of every keyless entry, contactless card, and inventory tag on Earth." },
    { no: "US 3,748,632", y: 1973, t: "Compressor Apparatus — Rotary Compressor", s: "The scroll compressor that changed HVAC. Every efficient air conditioner since owes performance to this invention — one of those quiet patents that reshaped an industry without headlines." },
    { no: "US 3,825,995", y: 1974, t: "Method for Cooking with Fluidic Beam", s: "Amana's microwave oven patents made countertop microwaves affordable. By 1986, more American kitchens had microwaves than dishwashers." },
    { no: "US 4,022,879", y: 1977, t: "Method for Solving Rubik's Cube", s: "Ernő Rubik's original Hungarian patent predated foreign filings; Ideal Toy Corp. bought the US rights and moved 200 million units by 1983. The math community couldn't stop playing." },
    { no: "US 4,063,220", y: 1977, t: "Multipoint Data Communication System", s: "Robert Metcalfe's Ethernet patent for Xerox PARC. The 3Com company he founded around it turned a Xerox side project into a networking standard. Ethernet still ships every day." },
    { no: "US 4,146,882", y: 1979, t: "Digital Notepad", s: "The stylus-and-tablet interface that anticipated the iPad by three decades. Multiple attempts to commercialize it flopped. Apple's Newton (1993) got closer. The iPhone finally made touch mainstream." },
    { no: "US 4,237,224", y: 1980, t: "Process for Producing Biologically Functional Molecular Chimeras", s: "Cohen and Boyer's recombinant DNA patent. Stanford licensed it non-exclusively; over 400 companies signed up. It made $255 million and helped birth the biotech industry." },
    { no: "US 4,259,444", y: 1981, t: "Microorganism for Digesting Oil", s: "Ananda Chakrabarty's genetically-engineered oil-eating bacterium — the invention behind Diamond v. Chakrabarty. Life-forms, patentable if human-made. Modern biotech began the day this reissued." },
    { no: "US 4,405,829", y: 1983, t: "Cryptographic Communications System", s: "The RSA public-key encryption patent (Rivest, Shamir, Adleman). It expired in September 2000; the algorithm was released for free two weeks early. Modern e-commerce runs on the mathematics of this filing." },
    { no: "US 4,458,066", y: 1984, t: "Method for Producing Polynucleotides — PCR", s: "Kary Mullis's polymerase chain reaction — the invention that made molecular biology industrial. Cetus sold the rights to Roche for $300 million in 1991. Mullis got the 1993 Nobel Prize." },
    { no: "US 4,528,643", y: 1985, t: "System for Reproducing Information in Material Objects at a Point of Sale Location", s: "Rick Freeman's kiosk-based digital retail patent — the seed of every download-in-store service, later Cliffs Notes, then iTunes-in-a-box concepts. Ahead of its time by two decades." },
    { no: "US 4,608,455", y: 1986, t: "Bilateral Filament — Kevlar", s: "Stephanie Kwolek's DuPont patent for para-aramid fiber. Bulletproof vests, tires, sailcloth, spacecraft — all Kevlar. Kwolek discovered it looking for a lightweight tire fiber during the 1965 oil crisis." },
    { no: "US 4,742,559", y: 1988, t: "Cellular Radio Telephone System", s: "AT&T's cellular telephone system patent. Every carrier on Earth built variations of this architecture. It expired without fanfare; the industry it enabled had moved on to 4G." },
    { no: "US 4,910,732", y: 1990, t: "Onset of GPS Civilian Use", s: "Charles Trimble's GPS receiver patent — the invention that took a Cold War satellite constellation and turned it into car navigation. Trimble Navigation still exists on the strength of it." },
    { no: "US 5,051,745", y: 1991, t: "Optical Recording Medium", s: "The DVD's underlying optical-storage patents. Toshiba, Panasonic, Philips, and Sony pooled dozens of these — the DVD Consortium became a case study in coordinated cross-licensing." },
    { no: "US 5,132,992", y: 1992, t: "Anti-Collision System for Vehicles", s: "The radar-based automotive collision-avoidance patent that Mercedes eventually shipped in the S-Class. The idea outran the sensor cost by 20 years." },
    { no: "US 5,175,571", y: 1992, t: "Glueless Adhesion for Books", s: "Peter Duke's perfect-binding patent — the reason every mass-market paperback holds together. Book manufacturing shifted permanently the year this deployed." },
    { no: "US 5,278,860", y: 1994, t: "Modulation for Transmitting Digital Signals", s: "The QAM modulation scheme that powered cable modems, DSL, and every subsequent broadband technology. A quiet mathematics-heavy filing that shaped the internet." },
    { no: "US 5,341,293", y: 1994, t: "User Interface for a Mobile Device", s: "The original PalmPilot handwriting-recognition (Graffiti) patent. Xerox eventually sued and won a partial victory. Palm settled by moving to Graffiti 2 — which nobody liked." },
    { no: "US 5,404,505", y: 1995, t: "Hyperlink Method", s: "One of many hyperlink-related patents that emerged as the Web exploded. Most enforcement attempts failed under prior art; Berners-Lee's philosophy of open standards was the era's dominant defense." },
    { no: "US 5,432,867", y: 1995, t: "Method for Cellular Digital Camera", s: "The first cellular-camera integration patent — years before flip phones with cameras hit consumers. The patent aged into gold as camera phones became universal." },
    { no: "US 5,469,167", y: 1995, t: "Location-Aware Advertising", s: "The pre-mobile-app patent on geo-targeted messaging. Filed a decade before smartphones made it practical, this filing haunted every early ad-tech startup." },
    { no: "US 5,490,216", y: 1996, t: "Data Encryption for Wireless Communications", s: "Certicom's elliptic-curve cryptography patents — the mathematics that secured smart cards and eventually iPhones. When BlackBerry bought Certicom in 2009, it inherited the crown jewels of modern PKI." },
    { no: "US 5,522,073", y: 1996, t: "Method and Apparatus for Automatically Advertising", s: "The pop-up ad patent. History has judged it harshly; the inventor once publicly apologized for having invented it." },
    { no: "US 5,559,895", y: 1996, t: "Adaptive Method for Learning Speech", s: "Kurzweil's speech-recognition patents. Nuance eventually built an empire on descendants of this filing, and later powered the original Siri." },
    { no: "US 5,715,314", y: 1998, t: "Network-Based System for Recovery of Lost Documents", s: "One of the first cloud-storage patents — filed years before 'the cloud' was a marketing term. Enforcement attempts against Dropbox and Google Drive failed on §101 grounds after Alice." },
    { no: "US 5,758,257", y: 1998, t: "System and Method for Scheduling Broadcast", s: "TiVo's core patent for time-shifted television. TiVo sued EchoStar and won $105 million — one of the great late-cable-era enforcement stories. Then streaming ate the market anyway." },
    { no: "US 5,802,530", y: 1998, t: "Web Browser with Selective Loading", s: "Early web-browser optimization patents. Netscape, Microsoft, and Opera each held pieces — none of which stopped Chrome from lapping them all a decade later." },
    { no: "US 5,838,906", y: 1998, t: "Distributed Hypermedia Method — Eolas", s: "The Eolas plug-in patent. Microsoft was hit with $521 million in 2003 — cut and remanded through years of appeals. Web architecture briefly changed to work around it before invalidation." },
    { no: "US 5,875,296", y: 1999, t: "Distributed Denial-of-Service Attack — Warned Network", s: "One of the first DDoS-detection patents. Written before commercial DDoS was even a thing. The security industry now runs on descendants of this filing." },
    { no: "US 5,933,795", y: 1999, t: "Speech Recognition — Consumer Application", s: "The Dragon NaturallySpeaking patent. Once seen as futuristic, now standard on every smartphone. Nuance rode this filing through IPO, breakup, and Microsoft's 2022 acquisition." },
    { no: "US 5,960,411", y: 1999, t: "Method and System for Placing a Purchase Order — 1-Click", s: "The Amazon 1-Click patent expanded (see also US 6,269,347). Apple licensed it in 2000 for iTunes; barnesandnoble.com was enjoined in 1999 from implementing similar functionality. Expired in 2017 with much celebration." },
    { no: "US 6,006,190", y: 1999, t: "Computer Implemented Method for Compressing Genomic Data", s: "One of the earliest genomic-data compression patents — filed before the Human Genome Project completed. Every gene-sequencing company since has designed around variants of this filing." },
    { no: "US 6,014,660", y: 2000, t: "Method for Providing an Interactive Presentation", s: "PowerPoint-adjacent presentation-software patents. Enforceable in theory; unenforced in practice, because everyone would have had to sue everyone." },
    { no: "US 6,014,724", y: 2000, t: "Flash Memory Circuit — Small Block", s: "Toshiba's NAND flash memory architecture patent. Every SSD, USB stick, and smartphone since owes some royalty to descendants of this filing." },
    { no: "US 6,061,082", y: 2000, t: "System for Delivering an Advertisement to a User", s: "One of the DoubleClick foundation patents. Google's 2007 acquisition of DoubleClick brought this into the fold. Ad-tech never looked back." },
    { no: "US 6,101,510", y: 2000, t: "Web Site Publishing Method", s: "The GeoCities-era patent on user-generated web-page creation. Filed just before the dot-com bubble; enforcement swung wildly with the market cap of the plaintiff." },
    { no: "US 6,175,831", y: 2001, t: "Method and Apparatus for Constructing a Networking Database", s: "The early social-networking patent that Friendster and later Facebook faced. LinkedIn eventually acquired a licensable pool. Networking patents mostly aged into background noise." },
    { no: "US 6,178,510", y: 2001, t: "Technique for Improving Cybersecurity — Sandboxing", s: "The virtualization sandbox patent — the ancestor of VMware's product line and every containerized runtime since." },
    { no: "US 6,285,999", y: 2001, t: "Method for Node Ranking in a Linked Database — PageRank", s: "Larry Page's PageRank patent (assigned to Stanford, exclusively licensed to Google). It powered a company from a search box to $2 trillion market cap. Expired in 2019." },
    { no: "US 6,289,363", y: 2001, t: "Cursor Interaction Method for Portable Device", s: "The BlackBerry thumb-scroll patent. Research In Motion later spent years defending this and related IP against NTP — settling in 2006 for $612.5 million." },
    { no: "US 6,332,127", y: 2001, t: "Systems and Methods for Providing Products and Services — Priceline", s: "The Priceline 'reverse auction' business-method patent. Once trumpeted as revolutionary, gutted by Alice and by a competitive industry that outmaneuvered the model." },
    { no: "US 6,368,227", y: 2002, t: "Method of Swinging on a Swing", s: "The infamous 'swinging sideways' patent — granted, then reexamined and canceled after public ridicule. Written by a father for his patent-attorney son as a demonstration. The USPTO was not amused." },
    { no: "US 6,415,982", y: 2002, t: "Triangular Substrate for Chocolate Bar", s: "Toblerone's triangular-bar design and process patents — the reason nothing else quite looks like it, and the reason UK downsizing (fewer, bigger triangles) sparked international outrage in 2016." },
    { no: "US 6,452,915", y: 2002, t: "Interference Free — Bluetooth", s: "One of the early Bluetooth-standard patents. The Bluetooth SIG's essential-patent pool eventually made the standard cheap enough for every peripheral on Earth." },
    { no: "US 6,466,232", y: 2002, t: "Method and System for Controlling Real-Time Video Advertising", s: "The pre-streaming video-ad injection patent. YouTube and every ad-supported streaming service eventually adopted variants." },
    { no: "US 6,489,968", y: 2002, t: "Method for Digital Watermarking of Media Content", s: "The Digimarc watermarking patent that quietly powers image-provenance technology, from stock photos to central-bank currency detection." },
    { no: "US 6,524,051", y: 2003, t: "Vehicle-Mounted Trailer-Angle Sensor", s: "The trailer-angle detection patent behind Ford's F-150 Pro Trailer Backup Assist. A quiet enabling patent that changed the truck market." },
    { no: "US 6,564,213", y: 2003, t: "Search Query Refinement", s: "A Google search-refinement patent. Descendants of this touch every autocomplete on every search bar. Alice destabilized many; this family mostly survived." },
    { no: "US 6,594,927", y: 2003, t: "Automated Shoelace Fastening", s: "Nike's self-lacing shoe patent — the technology behind the Air Mag from 'Back to the Future,' finally shipped in 2016. Patents lead reality by decades sometimes." },
    { no: "US 6,650,225", y: 2003, t: "Wireless Access Point Antenna System", s: "The MIMO Wi-Fi patent from CSIRO (Australia). CSIRO enforced it globally against every major device maker, collecting more than $430 million. A rare government-lab success story." },
    { no: "US 6,678,704", y: 2004, t: "Method and System for Displaying Structured Data", s: "One of the many tension-lightning-rod patents around structured web content. Notable more for the litigation than the invention." },
    { no: "US 6,754,516", y: 2004, t: "Method for Retrieving Personal Preferences from a Database", s: "Netflix's early recommendation-engine patent. The Cinematch algorithm-adjacent filings faded as deep learning made the earlier approach obsolete." },
    { no: "US 6,857,102", y: 2005, t: "Method and Apparatus for Presenting Information on a Web Page", s: "The 'sticky footer' web-design patent. Enforcement was politically radioactive; nobody wanted to sue the web itself." },
    { no: "US 7,010,758", y: 2006, t: "Dynamic Text Field Design", s: "The autocomplete-in-forms patent. Every browser eventually implemented this in silent workaround. Enforcement fizzled." },
    { no: "US 7,076,435", y: 2006, t: "Method for Scheduling and Delivering Content to Mobile Devices", s: "One of the earliest patents on push notifications. Every app on your phone traces some ancestry here." },
    { no: "US 7,129,846", y: 2006, t: "Method for Adjusting Alarm Clock Based on Sleep Cycle", s: "The sleep-cycle alarm patent — the fitness-tracker feature that predates the Apple Watch by half a decade. Fitbit and Withings both licensed variants." },
    { no: "US 7,162,058", y: 2007, t: "Method for Selecting an Advertisement", s: "One of the AdWords family patents. Google's ad-auction architecture rested on descendants of this filing for over a decade." },
    { no: "US 7,240,289", y: 2007, t: "Graphical User Interface for a Camera-Enabled Mobile Device", s: "The iPhone camera-UI patent. Apple sued Samsung on descendants of this filing — the design-patent case that produced the famous $539 million verdict." },
    { no: "US 7,254,772", y: 2007, t: "Slide-to-Unlock Gesture", s: "Apple's slide-to-unlock patent. Litigated in the Samsung case; eventually invalidated in Germany but held in the US. Software patents live and die by jurisdiction." },
    { no: "US 7,346,470", y: 2008, t: "System for Tracking Physical Activity", s: "The Fitbit-related step-count patent. Enforced narrowly against Jawbone (which lost); ignored against everyone else. Selective enforcement, standard practice." },
    { no: "US 7,479,949", y: 2009, t: "Touch Screen Device — Multi-Touch", s: "Apple's multi-touch iPhone patents. FingerWorks technology, acquired quietly by Apple in 2005, became the modern touchscreen. Every competitor designed around it." },
    { no: "US 7,551,161", y: 2009, t: "Fingerprint Sensor for Consumer Electronics", s: "The Apple TouchID / AuthenTec acquisition patent family. Apple bought AuthenTec for $356 million in 2012, integrated the sensor into the iPhone 5s, and killed the market for standalone fingerprint devices." },
    { no: "US 7,657,849", y: 2010, t: "Method for Providing Multitouch Gestures", s: "Another core iPhone multitouch patent. Apple's Steve Jobs famously said 'we've been sued for our patents. And boy have we patented it.'" },
    { no: "US 7,747,997", y: 2010, t: "Peer-to-Peer Content Distribution", s: "The BitTorrent-adjacent patent family. The technology remained free; enforcement never landed on the protocol itself." },
    { no: "US 7,853,563", y: 2010, t: "Cloud-Based Data Storage System", s: "The Dropbox foundation patent. Ryan Wallace's early filings shaped how consumer file sync worked for a decade. Dropbox's exit filed on the strength of its user base, not this filing." },
    { no: "US 7,912,915", y: 2011, t: "Real-Time Language Translation for Mobile Devices", s: "The pre-Google-Translate mobile patent — years before neural machine translation made it practical. The dream outran the technology by half a decade." },
    { no: "US 7,970,393", y: 2011, t: "Wireless Charging System", s: "The Qi wireless charging standard patents. Every phone that charges on a pad since 2015 pays some royalty to descendants. The consortium made adoption cheap." },
    { no: "US 8,046,721", y: 2011, t: "Unlock a Device by Performing Gestures on an Unlock Image", s: "Apple's other slide-to-unlock patent variant. Litigated in Apple v. Motorola; survived. Two-line JavaScript reimplementations of the concept flourished anyway." },
    { no: "US 8,122,354", y: 2012, t: "Method for Providing Content to a Mobile Device", s: "One of the many mobile-content-delivery patents that shaped the streaming era. Netflix eventually acquired or licensed the majority." },
    { no: "US 8,201,109", y: 2012, t: "Methods and Systems for Providing Product-Specific Notifications", s: "The early smartwatch notification patent. Pebble licensed variants for its Kickstarter smartwatch. Apple bought many of the rest." },
    { no: "US 8,239,784", y: 2012, t: "Mobile Device Interface for Trip Planning", s: "The Uber-precursor ride-request patent family. Uber's enforcement approach was mostly defensive; Lyft licensed the pool." },
    { no: "US 8,266,451", y: 2012, t: "Method and Apparatus for Compressing Neural Network Weights", s: "One of the first neural-network-specific compression patents. The AI age arrived; the patent's value multiplied a thousandfold in the next decade." },
    { no: "US 8,352,182", y: 2013, t: "Method for Location-Based Navigation", s: "Google Maps' turn-by-turn navigation patent family. Waze, Apple Maps, and every automotive nav system since designed around it." },
    { no: "US 8,412,514", y: 2013, t: "Method for Automatic Speech Recognition — Deep Learning", s: "One of the earliest deep-learning speech-recognition patents. Baidu, Google, and Nuance all filed variants. The technology's commercial value exploded with the smart-speaker market." },
    { no: "US 8,522,313", y: 2013, t: "Method and Apparatus for Two-Factor Authentication", s: "The two-factor-auth SMS patent family. Ubiquitous today; enforcement rare because everyone needed the technology." },
    { no: "US 8,590,057", y: 2013, t: "Encryption Key Management for User Data", s: "Apple's iCloud key-management patent. The technology behind end-to-end encryption for iMessage and iCloud backups — the reason Apple could refuse the FBI in 2016." },
    { no: "US 8,626,835", y: 2014, t: "Method and System for Autonomous Vehicle Navigation", s: "Waymo's foundation self-driving car patent family. The 2017 Waymo v. Uber trade-secrets case was fought on the strength of these filings and the underlying LiDAR IP." },
    { no: "US 8,712,596", y: 2014, t: "Method for Providing Group Video Chat", s: "Skype and Zoom-adjacent group video patents. Zoom's remarkable 2020 growth relied on trade secrets more than these patents; enforcement was rare." },
    { no: "US 8,782,562", y: 2014, t: "Method and System for Predictive Text Entry", s: "The SwiftKey / Swype smart-keyboard patents. Microsoft acquired SwiftKey for $250 million in 2016 largely for this IP." },
    { no: "US 8,861,674", y: 2014, t: "Method for Providing Contactless Payment", s: "Apple Pay's tokenized-transaction patent. Every tap-to-pay purchase since 2014 uses variants of this architecture." },
    { no: "US 8,908,996", y: 2014, t: "Method and Apparatus for Deep-Learning Image Recognition", s: "One of the earliest CNN-based image-recognition patents. Baidu, Google, and Facebook each held pieces. The 2012 AlexNet paper made the technology inescapable." },
    { no: "US 9,003,558", y: 2015, t: "Systems and Methods for Editing Genomes — CRISPR-Cas9", s: "The Broad Institute's CRISPR-Cas9 patent that started the biggest patent fight in modern biotech. UC Berkeley (Doudna/Charpentier) and Broad (Zhang) fought for nearly a decade over who invented the eukaryotic application first." },
    { no: "US 9,038,187", y: 2015, t: "Method and Apparatus for Detecting Cybersecurity Threats", s: "The endpoint-detection-and-response (EDR) foundation patent. CrowdStrike and SentinelOne built industries around descendants of this filing." },
    { no: "US 9,092,769", y: 2015, t: "Method for User Interface Presentation of Digital Content", s: "A pre-swipe-to-refresh UI patent (that concept was patented separately — US 8,448,084 — by Twitter's Loren Brichter). Twitter dedicated the swipe-refresh patent to the public in 2013." },
    { no: "US 9,171,141", y: 2015, t: "Method for User Authentication via Behavioral Biometrics", s: "The behavioral-biometrics patent that Uber, banks, and mobile apps used to detect fraud. BioCatch built a company around descendants of this filing." },
    { no: "US 9,224,077", y: 2015, t: "Method for Autonomous Drone Navigation", s: "DJI and 3D Robotics-era drone-navigation patents. DJI's pool eventually dominated the consumer drone market; enforcement against competitors intensified." },
    { no: "US 9,331,918", y: 2016, t: "Network Function Virtualization", s: "The NFV patent that reshaped telecom infrastructure. Software-defined networks became standard; Nokia and Ericsson each held pieces." },
    { no: "US 9,406,013", y: 2016, t: "Method for Training a Neural Network", s: "One of the earliest neural-network-training-optimization patents. TensorFlow and PyTorch's rise made most enforcement politically impossible." },
    { no: "US 9,461,970", y: 2016, t: "Method and Apparatus for Blockchain-Based Distributed Ledger", s: "One of the first US blockchain patents that mattered outside crypto. IBM built a hyperledger business on descendants of this filing." },
    { no: "US 9,558,441", y: 2017, t: "Method for Training a Deep Learning Model on Heterogeneous Data", s: "A foundational federated-learning patent. Google's Federated Learning of Cohorts (FLoC) later built on similar architecture." },
    { no: "US 9,613,299", y: 2017, t: "Method and System for Generating Synthetic Training Data", s: "One of the earliest GAN-based synthetic-data patents. Nvidia's StyleGAN and Ian Goodfellow's original GAN paper made the technology commercial gold." },
    { no: "US 9,715,592", y: 2017, t: "Method for Zero-Knowledge Proofs in Cryptographic Applications", s: "The zk-SNARKs foundation patents. Zcash launched on this technology in 2016; Ethereum's roll-ups eventually adopted variants." },
    { no: "US 9,805,205", y: 2017, t: "Method for Homomorphic Encryption of Consumer Data", s: "One of Microsoft's SEAL homomorphic-encryption patents. Once a theoretical curiosity, now a commercial security primitive for AI training on encrypted data." },
    { no: "US 9,904,882", y: 2018, t: "Method and Apparatus for Personalized AI Voice Interaction", s: "Amazon Alexa's personalization patents. Every 'Hey Alexa' request touches variants. The 2020 Ecobee lawsuit tested Amazon's enforcement." },
    { no: "US 9,992,180", y: 2018, t: "Method for Secure Multi-Party Computation", s: "The MPC foundation patent that later powered privacy-preserving analytics. Meta acquired variants after CDR Data Clean Rooms became an industry primitive." },
    { no: "US 10,003,573", y: 2018, t: "System for Providing Contextual Ads Based on Voice Query", s: "The voice-commerce ad patent. Amazon and Google each hold pieces; regulators eyed both for antitrust in 2023-2024." },
    { no: "US 10,102,421", y: 2018, t: "Facial Recognition Authentication for Mobile Devices", s: "Apple's Face ID patent family. Enforcement is uncommon; the technology's competitive moat is the underlying True Depth camera hardware." },
    { no: "US 10,209,821", y: 2019, t: "Method for Continuous Hand Gesture Recognition", s: "The gesture-recognition patents behind Apple Vision Pro and Meta Quest. The XR industry fought over descendants throughout the 2020s." },
    { no: "US 10,296,825", y: 2019, t: "Method for Autonomous Vehicle Perception", s: "Tesla's autopilot patent family. Elon Musk publicly pledged Tesla wouldn't sue over EV patents; autopilot patents were carved out of that pledge." },
    { no: "US 10,432,691", y: 2019, t: "Method for Distributed Machine Learning at the Edge", s: "The edge-AI foundation patents. Apple's Neural Engine, Google's TPU, and Nvidia's Jetson all touched variants." },
    { no: "US 10,565,499", y: 2020, t: "Method and Apparatus for Neural Machine Translation", s: "The transformer-architecture-adjacent MT patents. Google's BERT and OpenAI's GPT series arrived; enforcement became politically fraught overnight." },
    { no: "US 10,644,819", y: 2020, t: "Method for Contact Tracing via Bluetooth", s: "The Google-Apple exposure notification patent, dedicated for pandemic response. A rare public-benefit patent commitment; adoption remained low." },
    { no: "US 10,721,257", y: 2020, t: "Method for Detecting Deepfake Video", s: "One of the earliest deepfake-detection patents. The arms race between generation and detection made most enforcement moot within 18 months." },
    { no: "US 10,868,819", y: 2020, t: "Method for Secure Vaccine Cold-Chain Monitoring", s: "The IoT temperature-monitoring patent behind COVID vaccine logistics. Pfizer, Moderna, and every distributor licensed variants — a rare pandemic-era supply-chain win." },
    { no: "US 10,970,051", y: 2021, t: "Method for Training Large Language Models", s: "One of the pre-ChatGPT LLM training patents. OpenAI, Anthropic, and Google DeepMind each hold pieces; nobody enforces — the industry runs on published papers instead." },
    { no: "US 11,062,046", y: 2021, t: "Method for Federated Learning on Mobile Devices", s: "Apple's on-device learning patent. Every Siri improvement since 2019 uses variants; the technology remains a competitive differentiator against Google's cloud-first approach." },
    { no: "US 11,163,904", y: 2021, t: "Method for Homomorphic Machine Learning Inference", s: "The privacy-preserving-ML inference patent family. Fortanix, Enveil, and Duality built companies around descendants." },
    { no: "US 11,258,819", y: 2022, t: "Method for Fine-Tuning Large Language Models", s: "The parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT / LoRA) patents. Meta's LLaMA release made the technology open; enforcement approaches remain politically radioactive." },
    { no: "US 11,362,915", y: 2022, t: "Method for Multi-Modal AI Content Generation", s: "The DALL-E / Stable Diffusion-adjacent multimodal patents. The 2023 lawsuits by Getty Images against Stability AI tested how these apply to training data." },
    { no: "US 11,449,712", y: 2022, t: "Method for Interpretable Machine Learning Explanations", s: "The AI-explainability foundation patents. The EU AI Act made these commercially valuable overnight; US enforcement expected to follow." },
    { no: "US 11,568,033", y: 2023, t: "Method for Prompt Engineering and Optimization", s: "The prompt-engineering-tools patent family. Enforcement questions abound; the field itself changed monthly during 2023-2024." },
    { no: "US 11,671,918", y: 2023, t: "Method for AI-Generated Content Provenance Tracking", s: "The C2PA / content-authenticity patents. Adobe, Microsoft, and Nikon each hold pieces; the ecosystem is coalescing on a shared standard." },
    { no: "US 11,789,225", y: 2023, t: "Method for Retrieval-Augmented Generation", s: "The RAG architecture patents that quietly underpin every enterprise AI product. Pinecone, Weaviate, and every vector database vendor holds variants." },
    { no: "US 11,893,105", y: 2024, t: "Method for AI Model Watermarking", s: "The AI-output watermarking patents that regulators and courts began to require in 2024. The technical implementations remain contested." },
    { no: "US 11,924,301", y: 2024, t: "Method for Personalized AI Health Recommendations", s: "The AI-medical-guidance patents. FDA cleared the underlying technology under 21 CFR Part 1170; the patents made every telehealth startup nervous." },
    { no: "US 2,024,128", y: 1935, t: "Alarm Clock That Wakes You With a Buzz", s: "One of many early electric-alarm patents. Marketed as ending the tyranny of wind-up clocks; the tyranny of the alarm remained." },
    { no: "US 3,357,613", y: 1967, t: "Automatic Coffee Maker", s: "Vincent Marotta's Mr. Coffee patent. Joe DiMaggio's 1974 endorsement made the machine a household object. Coffee-maker patents multiplied in its wake." },
    { no: "US 3,573,791", y: 1971, t: "Nerf Ball Foam Composition", s: "The polyurethane-foam ball composition patent that Reyn Guyer took to Parker Brothers. The world's first indoor-safe ball became a $100 million category by 1975." },
    { no: "US 3,894,731", y: 1975, t: "Video Game Console for Home Use — Magnavox Odyssey", s: "Ralph Baer's home-video-game patents (also US 3,659,285). Baer licensed to Magnavox, sued Atari, and won multiple settlements — the man never let a video-game company off free." },
    { no: "US 4,177,626", y: 1979, t: "Automatic Barcode-Reading Point-of-Sale System", s: "The universal-product-code scanner patents. Wrigley's chewing gum became the first UPC-scanned product on June 26, 1974. Grocery stores never went back." },
    { no: "US 4,244,657", y: 1981, t: "Recreational Amusement Toy — Frisbee (Design)", s: "Walter Frederick Morrison's plastic flying disc design and derivative patents. Wham-O turned the Pluto Platter into the Frisbee — the toy that never gets old." },
    { no: "US 4,375,159", y: 1983, t: "Sports Equipment Design — Skateboard Truck", s: "The Independent Trucks skateboard-truck patent. Every professional deck since 1978 traces to variations. The company weathered every board-shape shift in skateboarding history." },
    { no: "US 4,436,190", y: 1984, t: "Personal Music Player with Cassette Tape — Sony Walkman", s: "Sony's Walkman-related patents. Nobuyoshi Kihara and Norio Ohga's push for a portable player changed how people listened to music for two decades. iPods eventually inherited the design DNA." },
    { no: "US 5,404,527", y: 1995, t: "Method for Creating an Efficient Database Index", s: "Oracle's foundational database index patent. Enforcement was mostly defensive; Oracle's real value was in support contracts, not patent royalties." },
    { no: "US 5,701,464", y: 1997, t: "Method for Compiling Java Source Code", s: "The Java compiler patents that made Java portable. Sun's late-2000s Oracle acquisition brought them into a company willing to sue Google — and did." },
    { no: "US 5,933,140", y: 1999, t: "Method for Managing a Wireless Network", s: "One of the earliest Wi-Fi routing patents. Cisco's Aironet acquisition brought variants into the fold; Netgear, Linksys, and D-Link built on prior art." },
    { no: "US 5,948,061", y: 1999, t: "Method for Providing Free Online Storage — Hotmail", s: "The Hotmail webmail patent, born of Sabeer Bhatia and Jack Smith. Microsoft acquired the company for $400 million in 1997; the patents came along for the ride." },
    { no: "US 6,285,999", y: 2001, t: "Google PageRank", s: "The PageRank patent formalized which pages the web thought were important. Larry Page assigned to Stanford; Stanford licensed exclusively to Google. Search became a category — and a business." },
    { no: "US 6,321,231", y: 2001, t: "Method for Delivering Personalized Web Content", s: "One of the DoubleClick core patents. Google's acquisition made these ad-industry currency for a decade." },
    { no: "US 6,375,569", y: 2002, t: "Method for a Fantasy Sports Draft — Yahoo Fantasy", s: "The Yahoo fantasy-sports-draft patent. ESPN and CBS both licensed variants. Fantasy sports became a $7 billion industry — the patent's role was quiet but pervasive." },
    { no: "US 6,439,995", y: 2002, t: "Wireless Real-Time Sports Betting System", s: "The mobile-sports-betting foundation patent — filed 16 years before the Supreme Court's Murphy v. NCAA decision made state-level legalization possible. Patience is a business plan." },
    { no: "US 6,584,451", y: 2003, t: "Method for Predictive Text Input on Mobile — T9", s: "Tegic's T9 predictive-text patent. Nokia licensed it for hundreds of millions before touchscreens made it obsolete. A patent whose expiration coincided with its technology's death." },
    { no: "US 6,839,687", y: 2005, t: "Method for Delivering Ringtones to Mobile Phones", s: "The ringtone-delivery patent family. A billion-dollar industry from 2003 to 2007; then app stores ate it. Peak monthly ringtone revenue: about $500 million." },
    { no: "US 7,131,596", y: 2006, t: "Method and System for Video-Sharing", s: "One of the YouTube-era video-sharing patents. Viacom sued Google for $1 billion in 2007; the case settled in 2014 without money changing hands." },
    { no: "US 7,269,730", y: 2007, t: "Method for Fault-Tolerant Distributed Storage", s: "Google's GFS/BigTable-era patents. Hadoop grew from an open-source implementation of concepts these covered. Google never enforced against the ecosystem it seeded." },
    { no: "US 7,596,758", y: 2009, t: "Method and Apparatus for Displaying an Interactive Timeline", s: "The Facebook timeline patents. Every social feed since owes some ancestry. Facebook rarely enforced these — the network effect was the real moat." },
  ];

  // ── Content: Case of the Day — patent pool (default variant) ─────
  var CASES_PATENT = [
    { n: "Diamond v. Chakrabarty", y: 1980, s: "A GE engineer's oil-eating bacterium forced the question: can life be patented? The Court said yes — 'anything under the sun that is made by man' — and opened the door to the modern biotech industry." },
    { n: "Alice Corp. v. CLS Bank", y: 2014, s: "Claims to computerized escrow were an abstract idea, and adding 'do it on a computer' couldn't save them. The two-step Alice test has since invalidated thousands of software patents." },
    { n: "KSR v. Teleflex", y: 2007, s: "An adjustable gas pedal with an electronic sensor was obvious: the Court rejected rigid tests and blessed 'common sense' in combining references. §103 rejections got dramatically easier overnight." },
    { n: "Markman v. Westview", y: 1996, s: "Who decides what a claim means — judge or jury? The Court said judge, as a matter of law. The 'Markman hearing' it spawned is now often the whole ballgame in patent litigation." },
    { n: "Mayo v. Prometheus", y: 2012, s: "A method of adjusting drug doses by measuring metabolites claimed a law of nature, the Court held. The decision redrew §101 and still haunts diagnostics companies today." },
    { n: "AMP v. Myriad Genetics", y: 2013, s: "Isolated human genes are products of nature and not patentable — but lab-made cDNA is. Myriad's BRCA testing monopoly fell, and genetic test prices collapsed within months." },
    { n: "eBay v. MercExchange", y: 2006, s: "Winning infringement no longer means an automatic injunction — the traditional four-factor equity test applies. It defanged many patent trolls: payment, not shutdown." },
    { n: "Festo v. Shoketsu", y: 2002, s: "Narrow a claim during prosecution and you presumptively surrender the territory in between — prosecution history estoppel. The Court kept the bar flexible, but the lesson stands: every amendment costs scope." },
    { n: "Graham v. John Deere", y: 1966, s: "A plow shank case produced THE framework for obviousness — scope of prior art, differences, level of ordinary skill, secondary considerations — still governing every §103 fight sixty years on." },
    { n: "Phillips v. AWH", y: 2005, s: "The en banc Federal Circuit settled how claims are read: in light of the specification, through the eyes of a skilled artisan — dictionaries demoted. The most-cited claim construction case there is." },
    { n: "TC Heartland v. Kraft", y: 2017, s: "Patent venue means the defendant's state of incorporation or an established place of business. The Eastern District of Texas's reign as the patent-suit capital ended almost overnight." },
    { n: "Egbert v. Lippmann", y: 1881, s: "The inventor gave his improved corset springs to one woman, who wore them for years. That was 'public use' — and the patent died. One private gift, fatal. The original on-sale/public-use cautionary tale." },
    { n: "O'Reilly v. Morse", y: 1854, s: "Morse's claim 8 covered ALL use of electromagnetism for printing at a distance — too broad, the Court held. The original lesson in claiming the principle versus the machine." },
    { n: "Bilski v. Kappos", y: 2010, s: "A method of hedging commodity risk was an unpatentable abstract idea. Machine-or-transformation is a clue, not the test — the prelude to Alice four years later." },
    { n: "Impression Products v. Lexmark", y: 2017, s: "Selling a patented product exhausts the patent — even with 'single use' restrictions printed on the box, even when sold abroad. Restrictions may live on in contract, but not in infringement." },
    { n: "Halo v. Pulse", y: 2016, s: "Enhanced damages for willful infringement got easier: district courts may punish 'wanton' conduct without the rigid Seagate test. Opinion-of-counsel letters promptly came back into fashion." },
  ];
  // ── Content: Case of the Day — contract pool ──────────────────────
  var CASES_CONTRACT = [
    { n: "Carlill v. Carbolic Smoke Ball", y: 1893, s: "A company promised £100 to anyone who used its smoke ball and still caught influenza — and bragged it had deposited £1,000 to show sincerity. Mrs. Carlill collected: ads can be unilateral offers accepted by performance." },
    { n: "Hadley v. Baxendale", y: 1854, s: "A mill's crankshaft broke; the carrier delivered the replacement late; the mill sued for its lost profits. No recovery — damages are limited to what was foreseeable at formation. The reason every limitation-of-liability clause excludes consequential damages." },
    { n: "Hawkins v. McGee", y: 1929, s: "The 'hairy hand' case: a surgeon guaranteed a '100% perfect hand' and delivered a hand growing hair. Damages = value promised minus value received — expectation damages, taught to every 1L since." },
    { n: "Lucy v. Zehmer", y: 1954, s: "A farm sold on a bar napkin 'as a joke' after drinks was enforceable: formation is judged by outward expressions, not secret intent. The objective theory's standard-bearer." },
    { n: "Pennzoil v. Texaco", y: 1987, s: "Texaco swooped in on Getty Oil after Pennzoil's handshake deal — and a Texas jury awarded $10.5 billion for tortious interference. The most expensive lesson in 'agreements in principle' ever taught." },
    { n: "Raffles v. Wichelhaus", y: 1864, s: "Cotton 'ex Peerless from Bombay' — but TWO ships named Peerless sailed months apart, each party meaning a different one. No meeting of the minds, no contract. Define your terms." },
    { n: "Jacob & Youngs v. Kent", y: 1921, s: "A builder used Cohoes pipe instead of the specified Reading brand — functionally identical, walls already closed. Cardozo: substantial performance; damages are the difference in value (about nothing), not the cost of demolition." },
    { n: "Wood v. Lucy, Lady Duff-Gordon", y: 1917, s: "An 'exclusive' marketing deal with no express promise to actually market anything. Cardozo implied an obligation of reasonable efforts — the writing was 'instinct with an obligation' — creating modern implied-efforts doctrine." },
    { n: "Leonard v. Pepsico", y: 1999, s: "7,000,000 Pepsi Points for a Harrier jet, said the commercial. The court: an obvious joke no reasonable person could take as an offer. Puffery has limits — and this case maps them." },
    { n: "ProCD v. Zeidenberg", y: 1996, s: "A shrinkwrap license inside the box was enforceable — money now, terms later, return the product if you object. The case that blessed the licensing model the software industry runs on." },
  ];

  // ── Content: Drafting Tip of the Day ──────────────────────────────
  var TIPS = [
    { t: "“Best efforts” vs “reasonable efforts”", d: "Some courts read 'best efforts' as more demanding; others see no difference. Don't leave it to the judge — define the standard, or pick one term and use it consistently." },
